output.text:
{
  "103:1": "وَٱلْعَصْرِ",
  "103:2": "إِنَّ ٱلْإِنسَٰنَ لَفِى خُسْرٍ",
  "103:3": "إِلَّا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلْحَقِّ وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلصَّبْرِ"
}

{"connection_id": "BC-dfe6e8f7ed394b75fe9b", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.13.6", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "love rejoices with the truth", "note": "Paul says love does not rejoice in wrongdoing but rejoices with truth; this gives the commentary's haqq a communal ethical setting, where truth marks the difference between right and wrong action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5d3ea4ed14eddafb204e", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.13.7", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "love bears and endures all things", "note": "Paul describes love as bearing and enduring all things; this supplies a relational form of sabr alongside the commentary's reciprocal counsel, distinct from mere aim or force."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5c201b776307ff1fe7e5", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.58", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "labor not in vain", "note": "The commentary juxtaposes universal loss with directed good work; Paul urges steadfast abundance in the Lord's work because such labor is not in vain, specifying the exception to futility."}
{"connection_id": "BC-300e7967a325a16f13dc", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.9.26", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "running without aim and boxing the air rejected", "note": "Paul says he does not run aimlessly or box as one beating air; this names the wasted, off-target action that the commentary uses khusr to evoke, and makes disciplined direction the condition for effective effort."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c87912d5b751f0114faa", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.9.26", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "blow that does not miss", "note": "The commentary's worked spear and unswerving thrust make accuracy central; Paul says he boxes without beating the air, turning directed effort into a test of disciplined action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bc2db5d41aa2e516c297", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.2.21", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "no lie from truth", "note": "The commentary sets haqq against the false that it destroys; the letter tells its addressees that no lie comes from truth, making their opposition categorical."}
{"connection_id": "BC-42e76fc452c904157bb7", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.4.6", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "truth versus error", "note": "The commentary's claim that after truth comes deviation has a precise alternative here: receptivity to apostolic testimony distinguishes the spirit of truth from the spirit of error."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8bbf3edf31f46b330f88", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.2.25", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "straying sheep return", "note": "The commentary makes loss a move off the line; Peter tells his addressees they had been straying like sheep but have now returned to the shepherd of their souls."}
{"connection_id": "BC-986cf469a59f63b204df", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.4.19", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "suffering while doing good", "note": "The verse's exception combines righteous deeds and patience; Peter asks those who suffer in accordance with God's will to entrust themselves to the faithful Creator while continuing to do good."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1268e1316d3a2390fed1", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.5.14", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "admonish, encourage, help, and be patient with all", "note": "Paul assigns mutual correction and encouragement for different members of the community, then patience toward all; this closely joins the commentary's reciprocal counsel with its sabr, while spelling out whom that care serves."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c9c05d301d3706e6090c", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.5.14", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "admonish with patience", "note": "The verse joins reciprocal counsel and sabr; Paul asks the community to admonish the idle, encourage the fainthearted, help the weak and be patient with everyone."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3158d658c4b2c8554225", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.6.11", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "pursue faith righteousness endurance", "note": "The commentary treats right action as force aimed on a line; Timothy is told to pursue righteousness, faith and endurance, putting the verse's qualities into directed motion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-82d90d804aef8b185beb", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.10.4", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "weapons demolish strongholds", "note": "Paul's weapons are powerful for demolishing strongholds; this gives the commentary's haqq-against-batil image an inward, nonmaterial battle setting rather than a physical spear."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9f302b5e09ca0ba0f5d4", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.10.4", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "weapons demolish strongholds", "note": "The commentary presents truth as an impact that breaks falsehood; Paul's weapons have divine power to demolish strongholds in an argument over knowledge and obedience."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c33c4463a2d1bb3ae2e1", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.10.5", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "arguments pulled down and thoughts taken captive", "note": "Paul names arguments raised against knowledge of God and says thoughts are taken captive; this specifies the inner target of the weapons in the preceding verse, analogous to the commentary's blow that reaches inward."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4ab77d662ff60fe7572f", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.10.5", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "arguments brought down", "note": "The commentary says haqq reaches and overcomes its opposing target; Paul describes demolishing arguments raised against knowledge of God and bringing thoughts captive, specifying the intellectual target of the preceding weapons."}
{"connection_id": "BC-de0311ab90609ee9c2a1", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.6.7", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word of truth and righteous weapons", "note": "The commentary lets truth function as a directed weapon; Paul places the word of truth and God's power beside weapons of righteousness in his account of ministry."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0c7e4fedfdb118092523", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Pet.2.15", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "forsaken straight way", "note": "The commentary interprets loss as leaving the line; the false teachers have abandoned the straight way and gone astray, tying moral error to misdirected travel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0a22eca8df5f694ed588", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Thess.1.4", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faith and endurance under attack", "note": "The verse's believers recommend patience; Paul boasts of the community's steadfastness and faith amid persecutions and afflictions, giving sabr a social setting of pressure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2f04ab02af0429c51ad8", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.2.15", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "worker handles word of truth rightly", "note": "The commentary joins amal as directed effort to haqq as an unswerving result; Timothy is told to be an approved worker who handles the word of truth rightly and need not be ashamed."}

{"connection_id": "BC-0dce5f8af6e47d2400b1", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.4.2", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "preach the word, correct and exhort with patience", "note": "Paul tells Timothy to proclaim the word, correct, rebuke, and exhort with all patience; this closely combines the commentary's truth and reciprocal advice with sabr as the manner of correction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-afa6d4a9e94fb9f06fd9", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.4.2", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "exhort with patience and teaching", "note": "The verse couples recommending truth with patience; Timothy is instructed to proclaim the word, correct and exhort with complete patience and teaching."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1352423706764d6de8ba", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.2.37", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "listeners are pierced in the heart by Peter's proclamation", "note": "After Peter's speech, listeners are pierced in the heart and ask what to do; this gives the commentary's truthful impact an inward human response and connects penetration to changed action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-88731cbdafddda68438f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.2.37", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "speech cuts to the heart", "note": "The commentary's truth blow reaches within; after Peter's address the hearers are pierced in the heart and ask what to do, making inward impact lead to action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e4dbd838126e4f74d348", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.1.23", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faith does not shift", "note": "The commentary's faithful workers must keep from swerving into loss; Colossians conditions its hope on continuing in faith, established and steadfast, without moving away from the gospel's hope."}
{"connection_id": "BC-593e35ae39d198b3eec7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.3.16", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Christ's word teaches and admonishes people mutually", "note": "The community is to let Christ's word dwell richly as its members teach and admonish one another; this gives the commentary's mutual counsel to truth a word-centered communal form, with song and thanksgiving as its setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-50c49957de6508adc3bf", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.3.16", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "teach and admonish one another", "note": "The verse says believers mutually commend truth; Colossians asks that Christ's word dwell in them as they teach and admonish one another in wisdom, supplying an explicit communal verbal act."}
{"connection_id": "BC-335ae5e9f6d6468e5e22", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.4.2", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "patience, bearing with one another in love", "note": "Ephesians describes communal life through patience and bearing with one another; this gives the commentary's sabr a specifically relational practice, though it does not repeat its advice-to-one-another formula."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2eb60e2fe128b4dac93d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.4.25", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "truth spoken to each neighbor as members of one body", "note": "After rejecting falsehood, Ephesians commands each person to speak truth to a neighbor because they are members of one another; this is a concrete communal form of the commentary's mutual exhortation to haqq."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fd3926c92d34b35807d3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.4.25", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "speak truth to one another", "note": "The verse's tawasaw bil haqq is mutual; Ephesians tells each person to put off falsehood and speak truth with a neighbor because they belong to one another."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6f0d2c817b9aa51ab52a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.6.14", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "truth as belt and righteousness as breastplate", "note": "In Paul's battle equipment, truth and righteousness protect the body rather than serve as the commentary's offensive thrust; the shared pairing clarifies how the same virtues can be imagined as guarding against error."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9bf5e899805b2a9c64be", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.6.14", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "truth equips the fighter", "note": "The commentary's directed work and haqq meet in combat imagery; the exhortation has believers fasten truth around the waist as part of their equipment before naming the word as a sword."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a83c501658e6b68bd052", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.6.17", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sword of the Spirit identified as God's word", "note": "In the armor passage, the Spirit's sword is God's word; this makes the commentary's forceful action a verbal weapon, while Ephesians places it among the community's defensive equipment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-709bcc29de8bd5a99237", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.6.17", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word as sword", "note": "The commentary's directed work and penetrating truth meet the instruction to take the sword of the Spirit, identified as God's word, as a weapon in a struggle."}
{"connection_id": "BC-72c19975bcd5a2424a97", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.6.9", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "persevere in doing good", "note": "The verse pairs right action and sabr; Paul urges hearers not to grow weary in doing good because a harvest comes if they do not give up."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fb5e834b547794c9242a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.24", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "stir one another to love and good works", "note": "Hebrews tells its hearers to consider how to stir one another toward love and good works; this gives a specific communal practice corresponding to the commentary's repeated mutual exhortation and directed action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-759bffdd524ad8b2fad2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.24", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "spur one another to good works", "note": "The verse combines good deeds with mutual urging; Hebrews asks its audience to consider how to stimulate one another to love and good works."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b4ba9a56d71e6cb01174", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.25", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "meeting together and encouraging one another", "note": "Hebrews joins continued assembly to mutual encouragement as the day approaches; it adds the gathered setting for the commentary's reciprocal counsel and the persistence implied by sabr."}
{"connection_id": "BC-06d762d659fd445c6f40", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.36", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "endurance after doing God's will", "note": "The verse pairs action and patient persistence; Hebrews says the audience needs endurance so that, after doing God's will, they may receive the promise."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3c7d62732f5102688003", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.1", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "run the set race with endurance", "note": "Hebrews tells its hearers to run the race set before them with endurance; this combines the commentary's straight-course image with sabr, making patient persistence the means of staying on course."}

{"connection_id": "BC-f79ac56f0d35b03b1a67", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.1", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "run with endurance", "note": "The commentary ties directed action to sabr; Hebrews calls for endurance in the race set before the community, a sustained course rather than a single unswerving thrust."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aa1f44e9c01a21434413", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.13", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "make straight paths for the feet so the lame may heal", "note": "Hebrews commands straight paths for feet so the lame limb may be healed rather than dislocated; this combines the commentary's straight trajectory with its bodily penetration image, and turns straightness toward restoration."}
{"connection_id": "BC-05a8a87fd22e1e09220d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.3.13", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "daily mutual exhortation guards against sin's deception", "note": "Hebrews commands daily mutual exhortation so no one hardens through sin's deceit; this links the commentary's counsel in truth to preventing a person's deviation and adds a daily communal practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-34481171a4e2eaa5ca34", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.3.13", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "exhort one another against deceit", "note": "The verse's mutual counsel in truth prevents loss by deviation; Hebrews commands daily reciprocal exhortation so that sin's deceit does not harden anyone."}
{"connection_id": "BC-110acc68e00893baceec", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.4.12", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God's word sharper than a two-edged sword, reaching joints and marrow", "note": "Hebrews describes God's living word as penetrating to the body's innermost divisions and judging thoughts; it closely matches the commentary's strike that passes through to the inside, while locating the penetration in discernment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-67809dc8f506edd5f3ca", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.4.12", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word as piercing sword", "note": "The commentary imagines haqq as a blow that reaches inside; God's word here is sharper than a two edged sword and penetrates to the division of soul and spirit, making inward reach the point of the image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5ee9306d45ae8af9e891", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.4.13", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "inward exposure after piercing word", "note": "The commentary's blow reaches the inside; the continuation of the piercing word says no creature is hidden and all are exposed before God, identifying what the inward reach discloses."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e45f9d20ff2f35bd54b7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.3", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "testing produces endurance", "note": "James says the testing of faith produces endurance; this describes sabr as formed under pressure, giving the commentary's human target image a process of remaining steadfast through impact."}
{"connection_id": "BC-04e40fcaafb3a0116cb0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.4", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "endurance completes its work so one lacks nothing", "note": "James says endurance should complete its work so a person becomes complete and lacks nothing; this joins the commentary's sabr and amal to an explicit overcoming of lack."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9dfc016485f1cec2d9e5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.4", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "endurance removes lack", "note": "The verse's sabr belongs to the exception from loss; James says steadfastness must complete its work so the believers are complete and lacking nothing, joining endurance to the opposite of deficiency."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5a546eb4f32ed28d1308", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.2.17", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "faith requires works", "note": "The commentary turns amal into exertion with effect; James says faith by itself is dead if it has no works, giving the verse's faith and righteous action an explicit relation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ca13a9b5c1df96049974", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.2.22", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "faith works together with deeds", "note": "The exception joins believing and doing; James says Abraham's faith was active together with his works and completed by them, making the pairing operative rather than merely adjacent."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e642a7083764ad0a27ef", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.3.6", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "tongue as destroying fire", "note": "The commentary's effective truth is cast against its opponent; James calls the tongue a fire that stains the body and sets a life's course ablaze, a contrary example of speech as uncontrolled destructive force."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0689c5f851e7e8d30b8a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.19", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "one among the community wanders from the truth and is brought back", "note": "James describes a believer wandering from truth and another person bringing him back; this closely joins the commentary's deviation from the line to its mutual counsel in haqq."}
{"connection_id": "BC-48cb7662d22cff375e25", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.20", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "turning a sinner back saves a soul from death", "note": "James says the person who turns a sinner back from his erring way saves him from death; this makes correction of deviation a concrete rescue from loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3c13d59f09c659670ba9", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.14.6", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "way and truth joined", "note": "The commentary joins haqq with the path that avoids deviation; Jesus calls himself the way and the truth and the life, locating true direction in a person in this passage."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ccd9097c99b9a4368e49", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.17.17", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God's word identified as truth", "note": "The commentary's haqq functions through a forceful utterance; Jesus asks that his disciples be sanctified in truth and identifies God's word as truth, giving the verbal and moral terms a direct link."}
{"connection_id": "BC-716d1f0ac61de644e5b2", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.19.34", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a soldier pierces Jesus' side with a spear", "note": "John recounts a soldier piercing Jesus' side and blood and water emerging; this is a literal bodily counterpart to the commentary's lance entering the inside, while its passion context makes the pierced person a victim rather than a target of righteous counsel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9fc4ebf6c6dd041fde2b", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.8.44", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "lying speech and murder joined", "note": "The commentary opposes truth to falsehood as a forceful conflict; Jesus calls the devil a murderer and the father of lies, giving false speech a destructive agent rather than a neutral wrong turn."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b9eb65b24c7da0eaa4df", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.15.4", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "lost sheep sought", "note": "The commentary equates loss with going astray; the shepherd in Jesus' question goes after the one lost sheep until he finds it, giving the lost state an active remedy."}

{"connection_id": "BC-c9a14f368b436e7d09a8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.2.35", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sword pierces soul", "note": "The commentary's thrust penetrates inward; Simeon tells Mary a sword will pierce her own soul and thoughts of many hearts will be revealed, making interior wounding disclose what is hidden."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e0dcf5339508ab4e793e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.3.4", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Isaiah's wilderness oracle assigned to John's preaching", "note": "Luke introduces the Isaiah quotation as the word of God coming to John in the wilderness; this Christian reception relocates the road-preparation image into John's public call, complementing the straightened-path detail in the following verse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dfd17735b4f401bbba40", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.3.5", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Isaiah's crooked road applied to John's call to prepare", "note": "Luke applies Isaiah's road-making language to John's wilderness preaching: crooked ways are made straight as people prepare for the Lord. This Christian reading turns the commentary's khusr-as-deviation into a call for moral preparation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-10a7f58f4ad5c430f9d2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.8.15", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word held fast, fruit borne with patience", "note": "In Jesus' explanation of the good soil, hearers hold fast the word and bear fruit with patience; this joins inward reception, good action, and sabr in one image, adding a fruitful alternative to the commentary's missed or misdirected strike."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e1b7bcaf95983b661bb4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.12", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "one sheep goes astray", "note": "The commentary's khusr is deviation; Jesus describes a sheep that has strayed and the owner leaving the ninety nine to seek it, making recovery the response to lost direction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ef2e2d609cf7988c4a81", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.13", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the broad road leads to destruction", "note": "Jesus describes a broad way leading to destruction; this makes the commentary's khusr-as-deviation a path with a stated end, and contrasts the many who take it with the ayah's exception."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b152c5251b4c3fd85f57", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.14", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the narrow way leads to life and few find it", "note": "Jesus contrasts the difficult way leading to life, found by few, with destruction's broad way; this adds the exception's narrow, directed course to the commentary's loss-as-deviation image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d666b46bd1b3ec3d94eb", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.2.16", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "word held through labor without vain result", "note": "The commentary contrasts lost effort with a true directed word; Paul asks the community to hold fast the word of life so he can say he neither ran nor labored in vain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a16ad04d8d8a204346d4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.3.14", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "press toward goal", "note": "The commentary's amal is exertion aimed at a target; Paul presses on toward the goal for the prize of God's call, making directed effort explicit without a striking weapon."}
