{"connection_id": "BC-8116f257a2a0710da8c6", "ref": "WLC:Ps.64.5", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "arrows hit the innocent", "note": "The commentary's unswerving strike serves haqq; the conspirators shoot suddenly at the blameless without fear, giving the preceding verbal arrows an innocent human target."}
{"connection_id": "BC-32ceefcae2f10fc14498", "ref": "WLC:Ps.64.8", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God's sudden arrow wounds", "note": "The psalm answers the attackers with God's arrow suddenly wounding them; this supplies a divine counterstroke against corrupt speech, akin to the commentary's haqq overcoming batil."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3c826ca55a9765c295fd", "ref": "WLC:Ps.8.5", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "source word ٱلْإِنسَٰن root ء ن س / Hebrew אֱנוֹשׁ; table correspondence, BDB cites Arabic cognate", "note": "The source ayah labels al-insan with root ء ن س; Psalm 8 asks why God remembers enosh and attends to him, giving the shared human term a scene of divine care that complicates the commentary's human-as-target and loss image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1c5713455f5ef93ad462", "ref": "WLC:Ps.8.5", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء ن س / אנש; table sound correspondence, BDB note", "note": "The commentary places insan in a vulnerable human position; the psalm asks what Hebrew enosh is that God remembers him, emphasizing how small a mortal is within the larger creation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e7da1fcd33793413e377", "ref": "WLC:Ps.8.6", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "human crowned with glory and given dominion", "note": "The psalm says God crowns the human with glory and honor and gives him rule over the works of God's hands; this is a specific exalted counter-image to the commentary's target-like human and the ayah's general loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-675b856e404c4e2cec60", "ref": "WLC:Ps.85.12", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "truth rises from earth", "note": "The commentary's truth is thrown as an effective force; the psalm says truth springs from the earth while righteousness looks from heaven, the verse Genesis Rabbah 8:5 uses after Truth is cast down."}
{"connection_id": "BC-656dc7460e14a8d88dd7", "ref": "WLC:Ps.86.11", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "walk in truth on taught way", "note": "The commentary joins haqq to a course that avoids deviation; the psalmist asks to be taught God's way so that he may walk in God's truth, putting truth and directed travel in one prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ee988de30ff057b27f12", "ref": "WLC:Zech.9.14", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "YHWH's arrow flashes like lightning", "note": "As YHWH appears to defend his people, his arrow goes out like lightning; this gives the commentary's divinely cast haqq a projectile image, with national deliverance as its setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2f8ac1472b7bb6c28f0d", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 18:15-16", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "all-powerful word as sharp sword in Egypt's judgment", "note": "In the Passover judgment, God's word leaps into the doomed land as a stern warrior carrying a sharp sword and fills it with death; this gives the commentary's cast haqq a personified verbal weapon and a deliverance-through-judgment context."}
{"connection_id": "BC-02b7d9b841b38e5e88be", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 5:17-20", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "justice as armor; wrath sharpened as a sword", "note": "In its judgment scene, Wisdom clothes God in righteousness and justice and sharpens wrath as a sword; this gives the commentary's moral conflict a divine-war image while assigning the blade to judgment rather than to haqq itself."}

